refentry.refsect1:
&reftitle.description;
intfalsesocket_recvfrom Socketsocket stringdata intlength intflags stringaddress intport&null;
The socket_recvfrom function receives up to length bytes of data in data using socket, and sets address and port (if the socket is not of type AF_UNIX) to the address and the port of the sender. socket_recvfrom can be used to gather data from both connected and unconnected sockets. Additionally, one or more flags can be specified to modify the behaviour of the function. A datagram longer than length is cut, and the rest of it is lost.
The address and port must be passed by reference. If the socket is not connection-oriented, address will be set to the internet protocol address of the remote host or the path to the UNIX socket. If the socket is connection-oriented, address is "0.0.0.0" ("::" for AF_INET6) and port is 0, or an empty string for an AF_UNIX socket. Additionally, the port will contain the port of the remote host in the case of an unconnected AF_INET or AF_INET6 socket.
&note.bin-safe;

&reftitle.returnvalues;
socket_recvfrom returns the number of bytes received, or &false; if there was an error. The actual error code can be retrieved by calling socket_last_error. This error code may be passed to socket_strerror to get a textual explanation of the error. No error code is set when length is not greater than 0, and no warning is emitted when the call would block on a non-blocking socket. On failure, data, address and port are left unchanged.
At the end of the stream of a connection-oriented socket, or for an empty datagram, 0 is returned and data is set to an empty string.

&reftitle.errors;


 An ArgumentCountError is thrown if port is omitted
 with an AF_INET or AF_INET6 socket.

  &reftitle.examples;

   socket_recvfrom example


<?php
$socket = socket_create(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, SOL_UDP);
socket_bind($socket, '127.0.0.1', 0);
socket_getsockname($socket, $address, $port);

$sender = socket_create(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, SOL_UDP);
socket_sendto($sender, 'Hello, world! How are you?', 26, 0, $address, $port);

socket_recvfrom($socket, $buf, 12, 0, $from, $fromPort);
echo "Received $buf from remote address $from and remote port $fromPort" . PHP_EOL;


   &example.outputs.similar;


Received Hello, world from remote address 127.0.0.1 and remote port 39712




    This example creates a UDP socket on 127.0.0.1, sends it a datagram of 26 bytes, and prints the
    first 12 bytes received. The rest of the datagram is lost.
